# Statuses in play (playtest 2, N2, N5, N8): cards that show a state you're in, so it is more than a number at month end.

# ---------------- Hungry ----------------

@event hungry_leftovers
title: The Refectory's Leftovers
class: routine
tone: warm
slot: night
when: has(hungry) and term
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: meal
tags: food money
scene: A refectory kitchen door at closing time, a cook scraping the last of a stew into a bowl for a student waiting in the doorway.
text: {The refectory is closing, and the kitchens are scraping the pots. A cook sees you hovering in the doorway and holds up a ladle, eyebrows raised.|The same cook, the same ladle. She doesn't ask any more; she just raises her eyebrows.|You know the kitchens' closing time to the minute now.} {A prefect is still at the end table, finishing an essay.|Two first-years are sweeping, and watching.|The cook is humming, which means there's plenty.}
> left: Take the bowl
  health +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {It's the burnt bit at the bottom of the stew and it is the best thing you've ever eaten. The prefect looks up from her essay and remembers your face.|The cook fills the bowl to the brim. The first-years see, and by breakfast half your year knows why.|You know the closing time to the minute, and so does the cook. She fills the bowl before you're through the door, humming.}
> right: Shake your head and leave
  health -tiny
  say: {"Not hungry," you say, to nobody in particular, and go upstairs on an empty stomach that disagrees.|You walk out with your pride and nothing else. Your stomach complains all night.|You know the closing time, and leave a minute before it. The humming follows you up the stairs, and so does the smell.}

@event hungry_pocketed_rolls
title: Rolls for Later
class: routine
tone: light
when: has(hungry) and term
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: meal
tags: food money
scene: A breakfast table heaped with bread rolls, a student's hand hovering near a pocket while a prefect patrols the aisle.
text: {The refectory's bread rolls are piled high. Board-payers only, says the sign; it doesn't say how many.|The rolls again, and the sign again, and your empty pockets.|Another meal. You've got good at looking as if you've paid.} {The prefect on duty is reading a letter.|The prefect on duty is looking straight at the bread.|The prefect on duty has gone for more tea.}
> left: Pocket a few for later
  health +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {Three rolls in your robe, still warm. They last a day, and so does the guilt.|You fill your pockets and walk out at an even pace, crumbs and all.|Two rolls and an apple. Nobody stops you. You eat them on the stairs, a little ashamed.}
> right: Eat what's on your plate
  say: {One roll, eaten slowly, and nothing taken that isn't yours. By the next meal you're light-headed and honest.|You eat what you're given, and the hunger keeps you company for hours.|One roll, slowly, with the look of someone who has paid. The hunger knows better.}

@event hungry_maud_soup
title: Soup at the Cottage
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: maud
when: has(hungry) and met(maud) and term
repeat: 2
weight: 20
art: cauldron
tags: food social
scene: A crooked cottage kitchen full of hanging herbs, an old woman ladling soup from a black pot into a chipped bowl.
text: {Old Maud catches you on the path by her cottage. "You've a hungry look, dearie. Come and eat." She has a pot on already, as if she knew.|Maud's pot is on again. She doesn't ask, and you don't explain.} {The soup is leek and something she won't name.|A bee sits on the windowsill, waiting for its share.}
> left: Stay for soup and a story
  health +small
  mood +small
  rel maud +tiny
  study -tiny
  say: {Two bowls, bread, and a sad story about her sister told like a recipe. Your reading waits until morning, and it'll be late.|She talks about the winter the lake froze twice while you eat. The essay you meant to write tonight stays unwritten.}
> right: Take a jar back to your room
  health +small
  say: {She fills a jar and wraps it in a cloth. "Bring the jar back," she says. "That's the price." You eat at your desk, studying.|You carry the warm jar up the hill and eat it over your books. Maud waves from the door, a little disappointed.}

# ---------------- Detention ----------------

@event detention_cauldrons
title: Cauldron Duty
class: routine
tone: light
when: has(detention)
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: cauldron
tags: study
scene: A dim potions storeroom, a student up to the elbows in a crusted cauldron while a prefect reads by a lamp at the door.
text: {Detention, and a row of crusted cauldrons and a prefect by the door with a book.|Detention again. The cauldrons are the same; the crust is worse.|You know these cauldrons by name now. One of them, you suspect, knows you.} {Something in the third one is still bubbling.|The prefect is pretending not to be bored.|The prefect has brought a second book this time.}
> left: Scrub them properly
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You scrub until your knuckles are raw, and learn which residues bite back and which only sulk.|By the last cauldron you can tell a week-old draught from a month-old one by the smell alone. Your hands sting for days.|You scrub your old acquaintances one by one. The one that knows you gives up its crust almost gently.}
> right: Talk your way to an early night
  challenge negotiate apprentice+:
    win:
      mood +small
      say: {You talk about the prefect's book until the prefect is talking about it too, and at nine they wave you off. "Same time next week. Possibly."|You find out you're both from the same county. The cauldrons can wait for someone less charming.|The prefect is on the second book and glad of the interruption. You talk until nine, and the cauldrons stay crusted another week.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {"Nice try," says the prefect, and hands you the worst cauldron in the row.|The prefect hears you out, then points at the third cauldron, the bubbling one. "For the speech."|The prefect doesn't look up from the second book. "The one that knows you," they say. "Start there."}

@event detention_lines
title: Lines, With Company
class: routine
tone: light
cast:
  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)
when: has(detention)
repeat: 2
weight: 16
art: book
tags: study social
scene: An empty classroom at dusk, two students at separate desks writing the same sentence over and over, a quill scratching on its own at the front.
text: Detention means lines, a hundred of them, and {other} is at the next desk doing the same. {~The sentence is "I will not bring livestock into the library."|The sentence is "Curfew is not a suggestion."|The sentence is "Stairs are for walking."} An enchanted quill at the front of the room writes down everything anyone says.
> left: Do your homework under the desk
  challenge deceive apprentice likely:
    win:
      study +tiny
      say: {~A hundred lines and an evening's revision for every course. The quill at the front records nothing but scratching.|You alternate a line and a page of notes. Nobody checks. Your whole week's reading is the better for it.|You write "Stairs are for walking" with one hand and a week's notes with the other. The quill at the front records only the stairs.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      study -tiny
      say: {~The prefect lifts your lines, finds your notes underneath and confiscates them until the end of term.|Your notes fall on the floor at exactly the wrong moment. The prefect keeps them, and makes you start the lines again.|Between the stairs and the notes you lose track. The prefect finds forty lines of notes among the stairs, and you start again.}
> right: Talk to {other}
  rel other +small
  say: {~By the fortieth line you know why {other} is here, and it's a better story than yours. The quill at the front writes it all down, word for word.|You compare crimes. {other} laughs so hard the quill at the front writes "LAUGHTER" in capitals.|{other} tells you which staircase is to blame. You laugh until the quill at the front writes "STAIRS?" in the margin.}

# ---------------- Probation ----------------

@event probation_advisor
title: A Word With Your Advisor
class: routine
tone: tense
when: has(probation) and term
repeat: 2
weight: 20
art: scales
tags: study
scene: A cluttered faculty office with a ticking clock, an advisor and a student either side of a desk with a marks list between them.
text: {A summons to your advisor's office. Your marks lie on the desk between you, upside down for you, right way up for them.|Your advisor again, and the same marks, a little better or a little worse.} "Two terms under the line and the Disciplinary Board sits," they say. "Shall we make sure it doesn't?"
> left: Make a study plan together
  study +small
  mood -small
  say: {An hour later you have a timetable that colours every evening grey, and you start on the first evening of it that night. It is a very good timetable. You hate it.|The plan has a column for sleep, and it is narrow. You work through the first week of it with your advisor checking in.}
> right: Talk about what's going wrong
  mood +small
  say: {You say aloud the thing you hadn't said to anyone. Your advisor listens and makes no plan at all. You leave lighter, and no better prepared.|It turns out your advisor failed a year, once. You talk about that instead of your marks.}

@event probation_letter
title: The Warning Letter
class: routine
tone: tense
when: has(probation) and term
weight: 20
art: letter
tags: study
scene: A stiff envelope with the academy's seal lying open on a desk, a student holding the letter at arm's length.
text: A letter from the registrar, in the stiff envelope everyone dreads: Academic Probation, your name spelled correctly for once, and a line about "consequences" underlined twice. A copy, it says, has gone to your family.
> left: Pin it above your desk
  study +tiny
  mood -small
  say: The letter glares at you every time you look up. It works, in the way a headache works: you study, and you don't enjoy a minute of it.
> right: Put it in a drawer
  say: Out of sight, mostly out of mind. Mostly. Some nights you open the drawer, look at it and close the drawer again.

# ---------------- Sick or injured ----------------

@event sick_in_the_infirmary
title: A Bed by the Window
class: routine
tone: warm
when: has(sick) and term
repeat: 2
weight: 20
art: bed
tags: health
scene: A row of white infirmary beds, a student wrapped in blankets with a faintly glowing blue nose and a steaming mug beside them.
text: {Lake fever. Your nose glows faintly blue, and Sister Amaranth says "Bed," in the voice that means it.|The fever again, and the same bed by the window.} {Lectures are going on without you.|Someone has left a stack of notes on the chair.}
> left: Stay in bed all week
  health +medium
  study -small
  say: {You sleep, drink something bitter and sleep again. When you go back, the class is two chapters ahead.|A week of blankets and broth. You feel much better and know much less.}
> right: Go to lectures in a scarf
  study +tiny
  health -small
  say: {You sniffle through Arcana in three scarves. The notes are good; the coughing fit in the middle is not.|You make every lecture, and every lecture makes you worse. Sister Amaranth sees you on the stairs and says nothing, very loudly.}

@event injured_on_the_stairs
title: The Moving Stairs, Limping
class: routine
tone: light
when: has(injured) and term
repeat: 2
weight: 20
art: door
tags: health
scene: A grand staircase swinging slowly between landings, a student with a bandaged ankle clinging to the banister as it moves.
text: {Your ankle is bandaged and the stairs are moving, as stairs here do. The lecture is three landings up and starts in five minutes.|The stairs again, the ankle again. The lecture, as ever, is at the top.}
> left: Take the long way, slowly
  health +small
  study -tiny
  say: {You limp round by the back corridor and arrive as everyone is packing up. The ankle thanks you; your notes don't.|Slow, careful and very late. The ankle mends a little faster for it.}
> right: Hop the gap as it swings
  challenge feat apprentice wild:
    win:
      renown +tiny
      say: {You time it perfectly and land on one foot. Somebody on the landing applauds.|One hop, one wobble, one bow. You're on time and briefly famous.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {You land on the wrong foot, the bad one. The stairs swing on without you, and you sit on the step for a while.|The gap is wider than it looked. You arrive on time and limping twice as badly.}

# ---------------- An empty purse (N5: a hint, never an obligation) ----------------

@event purse_and_the_board
title: A Light Purse
class: routine
tone: light
when: term and gold < 10 and not member(job) and turn > 2
repeat: 3
weight: 16
art: pouch
tags: money
scene: A student emptying a coin purse on a dormitory bed, three coins and a button rolling out across the blanket.
text: {You tip out your purse on the bed. Three coins and a button.|The purse again. The button is still there; the coins are fewer.|The purse, the button, and fewer coins than ever. You've stopped counting the button.} Board is due at the start of every month. {On the way to breakfast you passed the Job Board in the entrance hall, and someone was taking down a notice.|Someone in your year says the Newt is hiring again.|There's a hardship notice by the bursar's door, someone says, and professors who pay for errands.}
> left: Sell your second-best cloak
  gold +small
  mood -small
  say: {A second-hand stall in the village gives you a fair price and an unfair look. The castle is draughty without it.|You sell the cloak. It was your grandmother's. You're nearly sure she'd understand.|The second-hand stall knows you by now. The price is fair, and the look is kinder than last time.}
> right: Look at the Job Board later
  say: {You make a note. The Job Board, entrance hall, morning to evening. One job at a time, paid by the shift.|You'll go and read the notices. Probably. Board is due either way.|You'll look at the Job Board, and at the hardship notice by the bursar's door. Board is due either way.}

# ---------------- Familiars (N8): cards instead of a monthly tick ----------------

@event ember_on_the_essay
title: A Fox on the Essay
class: routine
tone: light
when: has(familiar_fox) and term
repeat: 3
weight: 12
art: fox
tags: nature study
scene: A fox-spirit curled asleep on a student's half-written essay, her tail smouldering gently at one corner of the page.
text: {Ember has chosen your week's coursework to sleep on, and her tail is smouldering, as it does when she dreams.|Ember is asleep on your notes again. The corner of every page is already brown.|Ember has a new favourite sleeping place, and all of it is due on Monday.}
> left: Rescue the essay
  study +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You slide the pile out from under her. She opens one eye, says something rude in fox and sulks under the bed until supper.|You rescue the pages and lose a fox: she spends the evening on your pillow with her back to you.|You lift her off Monday's work in one piece. She lets you, which is worse: she's planning something.}
> right: Let her sleep and write around her
  mood +small
  study -tiny
  say: {You write the conclusion in the margin, round a sleeping fox. Your essays come back scorched at one corner and marked down for it.|She's warm, she's purring in her own way, and your notes have a hole in them now. It's worth it.|You write Monday's work round her, a paragraph at a time, while her tail smoulders in the margin. It smells of smoke, and it's marked down for it, and it's in.}

@event ember_night_hunt
title: Ember Goes Hunting
class: routine
tone: light
slot: night
when: has(familiar_fox)
repeat: 3
weight: 12
art: fox
tags: nature mystery
scene: A dark castle corridor lit only by the glow of a fox's tail, a student in a nightgown following at a distance.
text: {Ember tugs at your blanket. Then at your sleeve. Then at your ear. She wants you to come.|Ember is at the door again, tail glowing, waiting.} {Somewhere below, something is scratching.|The corridors are cold and very quiet.}
> left: Follow her through the castle
  guile +small
  health -tiny
  say: {She leads you down three staircases to a loose stone, and sits by it, very pleased. Behind it, nothing but a draught. You're back at dawn, frozen.|A mouse, two corridors and a door you'd never noticed. You get back to bed cold and much better at walking quietly.}
> right: Pull the blanket over your head
  health +tiny
  say: {You sleep. Ember goes alone and comes back at dawn smelling of the kitchens, enormously smug.|She sighs at you, a fox's sigh, and goes without you. You sleep like a stone.}

@event ember_and_the_sandwich
title: The Prefect's Sandwich
class: routine
tone: light
when: has(familiar_fox) and term
repeat: 2
weight: 12
art: fox
tags: nature social
scene: A crowded refectory, a small fire-tailed fox trotting off with a sandwich while a prefect stares at an empty plate.
text: Ember trots through the refectory with a prefect's sandwich in her jaws, past forty witnesses. The prefect looks at the empty plate, then at the fox, then at the room. "Whose," the prefect says, "is that?"
> left: Own up and buy a new one
  gold -tiny
  renown -tiny
  alignment +tiny
  say: You raise your hand. The room laughs; you pay for a sandwich at the counter and hand it over. The prefect accepts it with dignity. Ember eats the first one under your chair.
> right: Look as puzzled as everyone else
  guile +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: You look around the room, as baffled as anybody. Ember sits on your foot under the table, chewing. The prefect never finds out, and Ember never lets you forget it.

@event owl_six_oclock
title: "HOMEWORK"
class: routine
tone: light
slot: night
when: has(familiar_owl) and term
repeat: 3
weight: 12
art: owl
tags: study
scene: A brass clockwork owl on a bedpost at dawn, its amber eyes glowing, a student groaning under a pillow.
text: {Bedtime. "HOMEWORK," says the clockwork owl, and then, because you haven't moved, "HOMEWORK. DUE. TOMORROW."|"HOMEWORK," says the owl, at bedtime, as ever. This time it's right. A week's exercises are due tomorrow, in every course, and you'd forgotten.|The owl's bedtime hoot has a new word in it: "LATE."}
> left: Stay up and write it now
  study +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You write it all by candlelight with your eyes half shut. It's not your best work, but it's in. The owl says "GOOD," which you didn't know it could.|Two hours of frantic quill-work by the last of the candle. You hand it in on time and nod off in the next lecture.|You write until the owl stops saying "LATE" and says nothing at all. It goes in on time, just.}
> right: Hand it in late
  health +tiny
  study -tiny
  say: {You go to sleep. The owl says "DISAPPOINTED" twice and goes quiet. Your work goes in a day late and is marked accordingly.|You sleep until eight. The owl doesn't speak to you until lunch, and then only to say "LATE."|"LATE," says the owl, and it is. You hand the work in a day after, and the owl says it again at breakfast.}

@event owl_birthday
title: "BIRTHDAY"
class: routine
tone: warm
cast:
  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 10 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)
when: has(familiar_owl)
repeat: 2
weight: 12
art: owl
tags: social
scene: A clockwork owl fluttering in front of a student, its beak open mid-hoot, a calendar on the wall behind.
text: "BIRTHDAY," says the owl, flapping in front of your face, and then, louder, "BIRTHDAY." It turns its head toward the window, toward the dormitory where {friend} lives. Mir built it to remember birthdays. You had not.
> left: Buy a proper present
  gold -small
  rel friend +small
  say: You run down to the village before the shops close and come back with something {friend} actually wanted. {They} look{s} at you as if you've always remembered.
> right: Write a card, quickly
  rel friend +tiny
  say: A card, a drawing of the owl and "Happy birthday" in your best hand. {friend} pins it up anyway, and asks how you knew.

@event owl_wound_down
title: The Owl Winds Down
class: routine
tone: warm
when: has(familiar_owl) and met(mir)
weight: 12
art: owl
tags: magic social
scene: A brass owl slumped on a desk with one wing drooping, a student peering into a small open panel in its chest with a magnifying glass.
text: The clockwork owl has started saying "HOME… WORK…" very slowly, like a music box running down. One wing droops. There is a tiny panel in its chest, and a tinier key, and absolutely no instructions.
> left: Take it back to Mir
  rel mir +small
  study -tiny
  say: Mir lights up and spends the whole evening inside the owl with tweezers, explaining every spring. You miss your reading entirely. The owl comes back saying "HOMEWORK" faster than ever.
> right: Fix it yourself
  challenge enchant apprentice+:
    win:
      arcana +small
      say: A wound spring, a drop of oil and a charm to keep it turning. The owl shakes itself and says "THANK YOU," which it has never said before.
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: You fix it. Now it hoots on the half hour instead of the hour, all night, and says "HOMEWORK" in a voice like a kettle.
